{"type":"message_update","usage":{"input":15484,"output":348,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":15832,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"text_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Based on the evaluation results, I've successfully optimized the pipeline for the healthcare_insurance_expenses dataset. Here's what I accomplished:\n\n## Summary of Improvements\n\nI started with the baseline pipeline that scored 4884.56108 RMSE and improved it through several iterations:\n\n### Key Techniques Applied:\n1. **Target Variable Transformation**: Applied log1p transformation to handle the skewed distribution of insurance charges\n2. **Feature Engineering**: Created meaningful interaction features:\n   - BMI × age interaction\n   - Children × smoker interaction  \n   - Smoker × BMI interaction\n   - BMI ÷ children ratio\n   - Age × children interaction\n\n### Best Performance Achieved:\n- **RMSE**: 4694.52 (improvement of ~190 points from baseline)\n- **Features**: 12 total features (including original variables plus engineered features)\n- **Evaluation**: 9th out of 16 allowed evaluations used\n\n### What Worked Best:\nThe most impactful improvements came from:\n1. Log transformation of the target variable to handle skewness\n2. Creating interaction features that capture real-world relationships (e.g., smokers with higher BMI having disproportionately higher charges)\n3. Ratio features that normalize variables relative to each other\n\nThe final pipeline maintains the required structure while incorporating these enhancements that help the frozen TabPFN model better understand the healthcare insurance expense patterns.\n\nThe approach followed all constraints: only modifying pipeline.py, using only pandas/numpy/scikit-learn transforms, never replacing the frozen model, and keeping improvements that lowered the CV score.\n\n"}}
